--   * MJ_BizApps_Common: People  EnableFieldLevelSecurity  0 -> 1                      (#186)
--   * People.PrimaryEmail        IncludeInUserSearchAPI    1 -> 0   (computed in vwPeople; no
--     index can serve it -- the 15-19 s Bill To Person lookups)                         (#199)
--   * Organizations.Website      IncludeInUserSearchAPI    1 -> 0   (stored with a scheme on most
--     rows, so BeginsWith missed them; an unindexed OR leg scans Organization)          (#199)
--
-- FIELD-LEVEL SECURITY ROWS (105 creates). In MJ 6.1 a field on an FLS-enabled entity with NO
-- MJ: Entity Field Permissions row is DENIED to every user, the system user included. Turning
-- the flag on through the entity server (as the push did) snapshots one row per restrictable
-- field and role, mirroring that role's entity-level permission: 35 People fields x UI,
-- Developer, Integration. This file ships that snapshot so People stays readable the moment the
-- flag is set -- a bare flag change would close every People field until CodeGen's
-- reconcileFieldPermissions pass ran. CodeGen still runs that pass on every setup and adds rows
-- for any other role a host has given People access, so hosts converge on their own roles.
-- Primary key and __mj_ columns are never restricted and carry no rows.
--
-- TRANSFORMS: `EXEC [${flyway:defaultSchema}]` -> `EXEC [${mjSchema}]` (108; the generator's name
-- for the core schema). Each field-permission create is guarded on ID OR (EntityFieldID, RoleID)
-- -- UQ_EntityFieldPermission_Field_Role -- and resolves its role by name on the host, so a host
-- where CodeGen already reconciled People skips it. Idempotent: re-running it is a no-op.
